    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d503">

                <pb facs="0613" n="355" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg355"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 462.11Se8/12a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at London</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Skinner</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-03-23T17:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 23, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Endeavor ascertain owner cargo 
                        <hi rendition="#italic">William P. Frye</hi>, January 28, and any
                    subsequent change of ownership. Consul Queenstown cabled, “Understand 
                        <hi rendition="#italic">William P. Frye’s</hi>
                     cargo owned by Leetham’s of Hull, millers, who might or might not
                    have large army contracts.” Shippers inform Department cargo sold November 16 to
                    S. and T. Trounson, Limited, Redruth, England, but changed hands several times
                    in passage. Report fully, giving any facts suggesting Government interest.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
